    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Yamaha Previews the Future of the Tenere Adventure Bikes With Mysterious 4 Concept\nTesla Wants To Build 1 Million Cybercab Vehicles a Year in Germany\n13 photos\nPhoto: Tesla\nTesla is betting a lot of money on its German operation, where up to 1 million Cybercabs may be produced annually. Considering that robotaxis with two seats haven't established a large-scale commercial business anywhere on this planet so far, one has to wonder if Elon Musk is once again selling a vision ahead of the reality on the ground.\nIn any case, an estimated $3.3 billion is planned to expand Giga Berlin, a.k.a. the Grunheide factory. Tesla Cybercab production should begin there in 2029, and this investment could not have been announced at a more worrying time for the German automotive industry.\nAs you might have already heard, all three big German manufacturers are cutting jobs, reducing costs, and reconsidering their domestic production capacities. The Volkswagen Group, Mercedes-Benz, and BMW all blame Chinese newcomers and US tariffs, among others.\nTesla, meanwhile, is going in the completely opposite way with this investment. Betting that autonomous taxis will eventually justify an enormous manufacturing operation is definitely bold, and we already know that Elon Musk has lost a few bets over the years.\nFrom the Cybertruck 's projected starting price and commercial success to the overly ambitious FSD timeline, the SolarCity bummer, and the jumping Roadster Gen 2 that has very little in common with the concept from November 2017, the guy has been wrong about many things.\nIf you ask me, the biggest problem with Elon's latest bet is assuming that Europeans will be allowed to use the Cybercab as intended by the end of the decade. There's also the matter of European legislators, who are frequently criticized both in the EU and outside it for being slow and reactive instead of proactive.\nEU lawmaking does balance multiple institutions and different legal traditions, but looking at the bigger picture, the slower pace also prevents hasty mistakes while protecting the sovereignty of member states. As part of said €3 billion investment, Tesla plans a new production building at Grunheide.\nPhoto: Tesla The company intends to submit the building permit application before the end of 2026, as reported by Reuters. If the papers are in order and Tesla gets the go-ahead, construction should kick off in the first half of 2027.\nRegarding the Cybercab's European ambitions, bear in mind that suitable regulatory permission and insurance arrangements are required. Tesla also needs an operating network and auto-driving software calibrated for the European market, where both the infrastructure and the drivers are different from those in North America. As of this writing, the Cybercab is exclusively deployed for autonomous rides in Austin.\nDallas will follow suit. The wider Robotaxi network, which relies mostly on the Model Y crossover, operates across metro areas in Texas, Florida, and California. Turning our attention back to Europe, Germany introduced a legal framework for Level 4 automated vehicles in 2021. Under said framework, operators must obtain approval and restrict operations to designated areas.\nFully autonomous Level 5 vehicles aren't yet covered by EU law, yet a new global regulatory framework emerged in June 2026. As of November 2026, those rules haven't been incorporated into EU legislation.\nGiven this information, the Giga Berlin expansion is built on two suppositions. First and foremost, Tesla expects to navigate both Cybercab approval and operational requirements by the time production is expected to start in 2029. Secondly, the automaker further sees a European Union where demand for the Cybercab has the potential to hit 1 million registrations per year.\nVia // Reuters\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ntesla cybercab Tesla production Germany Europe\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Bentley x FRGMT Torcal Is a New Limited Edition Model",
